00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.040
When building gestures and
animations, the key to success

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.290
is to avoid frame drops.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.870
We want the user
interaction to run

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:03.240
at 60 frames per second,
which means that we only

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.080
have 16 milliseconds
to compute everything.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.610
And this is a very simple
diagram of the React

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:11.670
Native architecture.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:15.430
We have the JavaScript Thread,
which runs the react code,

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:18.900
and the UI Thread
which interacts

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.030
with all the native components.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.220
And they talk to
each other using

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.230
what we call the async bridge.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:30.480
They asynchronously send
each other JSON messages.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.520
So if your gesture or animation
relies on communication

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.880
between these two
threads, it is very hard

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.360
to guarantee that the
animation frame can

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.640
be computed within
16 milliseconds,

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.500
and you are likely
to draw up frame.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.790
There are a couple
of reasons for that.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:55.590
First, because of these
asynchronous messages

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.540
that needs to be
passed and serialized,

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.110
you might drop a frame
on the low end device,

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:05.350
and the JavaScript Thread might
be busy doing something else.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:09.480
For instance, processing the
response of an HTTP request.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.410
So maybe you have a gesture.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:17.190
So the UI Thread says to
the JavaScript Thread,

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.620
should I grant the gesture?

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.440
And the JavaScript
Thread might be busy

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.970
processing the response
of an HTTP request.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:29.760
So is not able to reply
within these 16 milliseconds.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.290
And you're going
to drop a frame,

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:36.630
and the user experience is
not going to feel very smooth.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.780
So the way we are going
to circumvent this problem

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.100
is by declaring all of our
gestures and animations

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.090
before hand, which
means that there

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:53.160
is no communication between the
JavaScript Thread and the UI

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:53.760
Thread.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.670
So it doesn't matter if the
JavaScript Thread is busy

00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:00.630
doing something else,
because the UI Thread is

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.630
going to be able to
do all the tasks he

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.400
needs to do for each frame.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.100
And plus, because there is no
communication between the two

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.570
Threads, we are sure that we are
able to compute the animation

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.930
frame within these
16 milliseconds.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.820
And I would like to
show you an example

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.940
of what this means concretely.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:26.340
So here I am dragging
around this component.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:32.690
And we implemented this example
using the default gesture

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:36.480
and animation API
provided by React Native.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.500
I call it a vanilla
animation APIs.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:48.470
And as you can see here,
the gesture depends--

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:52.070
relies on communication
between the JavaScript

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:53.750
Thread and the UI Thread.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:58.280
So to grant as a gesture, we
need to execute some JavaScript

00:05:58.280 --> 00:05:59.390
Thread.

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:06.060
When we move the
view around here,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.640
everything is done on the--

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:09.800
because we use these
animated events,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.600
everything is done
on the UI Thread.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.070
So there is no communication
between the JavaScript

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:18.320
and the Native side.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.680
And when we reduce
the gesture, we also

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:25.190
rely on JavaScript code.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:31.010
And so because we have
this mix of imperative code

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:36.620
and declarative code, if the
JavaScript Thread is busy,

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:40.850
this user interaction is
not going to run smoothly.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:46.790
And in fact, what I can
do is to modify the code,

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:51.140
so that we observe
JavaScript Thread--

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:52.820
we make the JavaScript
Thread busy.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:57.450
So I'm going to loop
5,000 time and maybe

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:04.100
like print something like
[? JS ?] Thread busy.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:11.180
And now, you see I cannot
move the ball around

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.030
because the JavaScript
Thread is busy.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.510
And so we are not going
to use these APIs,

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.880
because these APIs
rely on communication

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:23.840
between the UI Thread and
the JavaScript Thread.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:33.170
And what we're
going to use instead

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:39.740
is two libraries, React Native
Reanimated for animations

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:44.960
and React Native Gesture
Handler for gestures.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:48.800
And what these libraries
do is that they

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.900
enable you to implement all
of the gestures and animations

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:56.300
declaratively on
the UI Thread, which

00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:00.650
means that when you have your
gesture and animation running,

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:03.020
there is no communication
between the two Threads

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:04.410
that is involved.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:08.390
So here, if I make the
JavaScript Thread busy,

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:11.450
it doesn't matter
because there are

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:15.800
no communications between the
UI Thread and the JavaScript

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:16.850
Thread.

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:21.110
So this is the
same implementation

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:24.500
but using declarative
gestures and animations

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:27.740
using React Native Gesture
Handler and React Native

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:29.310
Reanimated.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:33.470
And now, so everything
is done declaratively.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:38.030
And now I can make the
JavaScript Thread busy.

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:53.250
So [? JS ?] Thread busy.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:56.430
So now the loop is
running, but because I

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.500
don't rely on the
JavaScript Thread,

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.340
you see I can still move the
ball around the smoothly.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.870
And so this is the heart
of the matter declaring

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:08.550
all the gestures and
animations before.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:11.100
And this will
guarantee us to build

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:15.640
user experiences that will
run at 60 FPS even on low end

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:16.140
devices.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.350
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:25.320
In this example, I
would like to show

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.010
you the power of
transitions in React Native.

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:31.340
Transitions are the easiest
way to animate components

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:33.440
in React Native,
and the way it works

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:36.560
is that we can attach an
animation value to a change

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:39.780
of state in a component.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:41.540
So here, I have a
very simple component

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:45.770
with a very simple state,
toggled is true or false.

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:50.180
If the toggle is
true, we apply here

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:54.800
a rotation, which is
index minus 1 times alpha.

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:56.750
So alpha is [? 30 ?] degrees.

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:59.810
Pi is 180 degrees divided by 6.

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:04.440
And here I do index minus
1, minus 1 times alpha.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:09.630
So if index is 0, 0 minus 1 is
minus 1 times [? 30 ?] degrees.

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.570
So we'll have a minus
[? 30 ?] degrees.

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:15.500
If index is 1, so the
count in the middle,

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.670
we would have 1 minus 1, 0
times [? 30 ?] degrees, 0.

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:21.860
So there won't be any rotation.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:25.760
If it's the [? third ?] card,
we will have index [? 2 ?]

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:28.640
[? to ?] minus 1 is 1.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:30.000
So we'll have [? 30 ?] degrees.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.070
So we see here.

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.260
Here we have the
cards constants,

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:35.280
which contains six cards.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:36.980
We select 3.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:39.710
And we calculate the rotation.

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:43.610
When pressing this button,
we toggle the state--

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:46.230
so to its opposite value.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:50.930
And so if toggled is false,
we have a rotation of 0.

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:55.640
And what we can
do here is to use

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:58.100
a hook called useTransition.

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:03.140
That will give us an animation
value that will go from 0 to 1

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:05.310
when toggled changes.

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.930
So if toggled is false, the
animation value will be 0.

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:12.050
If toggled is 1, the
animation value will be 1.

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:17.690
And the way the transition
from 0 to 1, 1 to 0 [? goes ?]

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:20.390
can be a timing function,
can be a spring function,

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:24.710
and can be any configuration
of the animation functions

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:26.180
that you want.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:29.060
So let's get this
animation value.

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:32.030
Here we're going to
call it transition.

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:35.750
And we're going to use a
function called useTransition.

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:38.660
useTransition comes
from a package

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:41.370
called react-native-redash.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:44.450
So we are going to use
react-native-reanimated

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:48.440
and React Native Gesture Handler
for the declarative gestures

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:49.640
and animations.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:51.410
And I created an
open source package

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:53.210
called react-native-redash.

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:56.990
I see it as being the
[INAUDIBLE] of Reanimated that

00:11:56.990 --> 00:12:00.140
provides us with a lot
of utility functions,

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:03.320
such as this useTransition hook.

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:06.980
But you don't have to be
intimidated by [? it. ?]

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:10.650
These are just helper
functions on top of Reanimated

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:11.720
and Gesture Handler.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:15.290
You can look at the source
code of some of these functions

00:12:15.290 --> 00:12:18.050
to see how it works
behind the scene.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:22.110
But these are usually very
simple helper functions.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.600
So this function
takes two arguments.

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:28.130
The first is the state
we want to transition on.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:30.320
And here it's toggled.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:36.150
And then we can pass an
animation configuration.

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:38.930
So for instance, by
default, useTransition

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:41.190
is using a timing function.

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:43.870
And maybe I want the
duration to be [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:45.960
You can set some easing.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:48.970
You can have a spring
function instead.

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:50.520
So here we have our transition.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:54.720
And what we want to do is
to interpolate the rotation

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:58.650
based on the transition.

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:01.890
So here, you see we
have the View component.

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:04.740
Here we use Animated.View.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:08.790
Animated.View is an
animation wrapper around view

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:11.640
so that in these
style properties

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.780
we can use animation values.

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:19.140
So here we pass a string, which
is a rotation value in radians.

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:22.140
But now because we
use Animated.View,

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:27.670
this style property can also
accept animation values.

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:32.680
So here, rotate is going to
become an animation value.

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:40.040
And we are going to interpolate
it using transition.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:47.190
So we can write it
using interpolate

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:49.050
from react-native-reanimated.

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:51.690
So the animation value
is [? a ?] transition.

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:55.960
Input range is 0, 1.

00:13:55.960 --> 00:14:05.270
And output range is if toggled
is 0, the rotation is 0.

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:10.330
If toggled is 1, the rotation
is index minus 1 times alpha.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:17.700
So I need to import interpolate.

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:18.900
Let's have a look.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:23.270
So you see here it nicely
transitions from one state

00:14:23.270 --> 00:14:24.390
to the other.

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:26.300
It looks very cool.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:30.020
Again, speaking of helper
functions in redash,

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:34.160
we are going to have a lot
of these animation values

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:35.300
that go from 0 to 1.

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:37.900
And we're going to interpolate
always from 0 to 1.

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:42.290
There is a helper function
called mix in redash.

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:44.990
So here we import
mix from redash.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:47.150
And it's the same
implementation as mix

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:51.440
in [? OpenGL. ?] So
we can pass directly

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.760
the output range like this.

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:58.130
So from 0 to index
minus 1 times alpha.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:02.010
So just a nice shortcut.

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:04.600
And here it is.

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:09.150
And the last thing we
can do is to change

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.610
the transformation of origin.

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:15.290
So here you see by default
is the center of the card.

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:20.540
And the way we transform the
origin in react-native using

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:25.070
the transform API is that we are
going to translate to where--

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:27.770
so from the center of origin
to where we want the new origin

00:15:27.770 --> 00:15:29.840
to be, do the transformation.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:31.370
And translate back.

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:35.970
So, here we want to translate
so the default origin is

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:39.890
half of the width of the card,
half of the height of the card.

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:43.280
We want to translate to minus
half of the width of the card.

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:44.600
Do the transformation.

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:47.960
Translate back to half
of the width of the card.

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:51.530
So we're going to
write translate x is

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:54.110
minus card width divided by 2.

00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:56.840
We do the transformation,
which is rotate.

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:58.730
And translate back.

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:05.590
So translate x is card
width divided by 2.

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:09.780
And so here you see
it nicely changes

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:12.060
the transformation of origin.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:17.970
And here again, we have a nice
utility function in Redash

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:20.410
to perform such a
transformation of origin.

00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:22.610
And it's called
transform origin.

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:27.090
And the first parameter is
the new transformation origin.

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:32.100
So here we have x, which
is minus card divided by 2.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.630
Y is 0.

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.100
We don't change it.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:37.070
And the transformation
is rotate.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:45.160
And so here it is.

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:50.930
So transitions are a great
way to animate components

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:56.110
in react-native with little
to zero knowledge of gestures

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:57.870
and animations.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:04.370
So just a great way to animate
changes in your component

00:17:04.370 --> 00:17:05.790
states.

