00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:15.790
In this example, we are going to
build a simple timing function

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:19.900
using some bare metal
APIs from Reanimated,

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:23.320
and getting to know
these low level APIs

00:17:23.320 --> 00:17:28.180
will enable us to build
incredible user experiences.

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:30.420
So here, I have a
chat bubble component

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:35.110
that takes a progress property,
which goes from 0 to 1.

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:37.210
So this is the state at 0.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:42.750
We can look at the
state at 0.5, 1.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:47.250
And so we're going to
go from 0 to 1, 1 to 0.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:51.150
And there is a state play,
which is true or false.

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:53.820
So we want this
looping timing function

00:17:53.820 --> 00:18:00.540
to be pausable and
resumable nicely.

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:03.680
And in order to implement
this timing function,

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:06.120
we are going to use
all the concepts

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:11.580
that react-native-reanimated
has to offer, or almost all

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:13.350
of the concepts.

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:17.070
And one of these
concepts are clocks.

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:21.090
So clocks are
animation values which

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:24.370
update themselves across time.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:29.400
So by default, the
value of a clock is 0.

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:33.390
You can invoke a function
called start clock on it.

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:37.740
And every frame, the
clock animation value

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.050
will update itself
with a timestamp.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:45.270
And this will enable us to
build animations across time.

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:49.140
Then there is a stop clock
function to stop the clock.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:54.620
So the clock animation value
will stop updating itself.

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:56.310
And there is a
function called clock

00:18:56.310 --> 00:19:00.060
running to check if the
clock is running or not.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:03.660
And so the way you can
create a clock is like this.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:08.820
So it's clock from Reanimated.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:14.460
We want here because we're going
to have many [? re-renders ?]

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:17.850
when the state changes.

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:20.430
We don't want to recreate
a new clock every time.

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:23.040
We want the clock
identity to match

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:25.300
the lifecycle of the component.

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:31.620
So we want to always have the
same clock for every instance

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:33.150
of our timing component.

00:19:33.150 --> 00:19:35.370
And so we can use a
helper function for that.

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:37.980
Again, from Redash,
which is useClock.

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:44.100
And here for progress we
can use an animation value.

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:49.940
So you would do new value
[? is 0 ?] from again,

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:51.080
React Native Reanimated.

00:19:51.080 --> 00:19:55.760
But we also want the identity
to be preserved across

00:19:55.760 --> 00:20:01.680
[? re-renders. ?] So we're going
to use useValue from Redash.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:08.010
And these are simple wrappers
using [? a lazy ?] [? useRef ?]

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:09.130
to have--

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:09.630
sorry.

00:20:09.630 --> 00:20:13.290
I need to import from Redash.

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:16.510
So what we are going
to do is that we're

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:19.840
going to use a hook
called useCode, which

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:24.460
allows us to declare
animation nodes to be

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:27.640
run for every frame
on the UI Thread.

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:31.840
So the function signature--
the hook signature-- is very

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:33.880
similar to use [? effect. ?]

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:37.150
The first argument is a
callback that returns here

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:38.980
an array of animation node.

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:42.070
The second parameter
are the dependencies.

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:43.450
Here we have none.

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:50.600
We want these animation nodes
to have also the same lifecycle

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:53.860
[INAUDIBLE] timing component.

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:57.670
And so what we're going
to do is use the clock

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.290
to control the animation.

00:20:59.290 --> 00:21:02.860
If the clock is not running,
the animation is paused.

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:05.620
If the clock is running,
the animation is running.

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:07.120
And so by default,
here, we're going

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:09.520
to want the animation to run.

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:12.580
So we're going to
use startClock.

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:18.040
And what we're going to do is
assign to the progress value

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:19.600
some timing function.

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:21.880
So some function
that we are going

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:26.390
to call runTiming and pass
the clock as parameter.

00:21:26.390 --> 00:21:27.860
So let's create it here.

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:34.420
So runTiming is a function that
receives a clock as parameter.

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:39.130
And we're going to return
some progress value.

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:42.560
Execute also a bunch
of animation nodes.

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:45.580
So you can use block
in order to do that.

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:50.440
Takes an array of animation
nodes as parameter

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:54.710
and will execute these
animation nodes sequentially.

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:57.220
So maybe I return 0.

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:01.590
So the last node in the array's
value [? that is ?] returned.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.010
And so you know--
you see here I'm

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:11.830
not writing progress
equals runTiming.

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:15.100
Because these are not
imperative instructions

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:17.830
that are run on the
JavaScript Thread,

00:22:17.830 --> 00:22:23.170
but declarative
animation nodes to be

00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:26.260
executed on the UI Thread.

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:30.610
So this is-- here it's not
a code that is executed,

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:33.220
but it's a declaration
for code to be

00:22:33.220 --> 00:22:35.110
executed on the UI Thread.

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:39.790
So we're not going to use the
if else syntax from JavaScript.

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:43.320
We are going to use the
condition animation node.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:46.540
We're not going to use equal,
but set animation node.

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:48.040
And so on.

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:53.260
So Reanimated provides us
with all the animation nodes

00:22:53.260 --> 00:23:00.430
we need in order to declare
complex animation states

00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:04.360
and interactions
on the UI Thread.

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:10.100
So lets write our
runTiming function.

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:15.630
So I need to import this
one from Reanimated.

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:19.010
So if we look at the
timing function from

00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:22.110
react-native-reanimated,
we see that it takes three

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:28.720
parameters-- the clock, so
to you know, see where--

00:23:28.720 --> 00:23:35.270
how we evolved across
time, the animation state.

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:37.020
So finished position.

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:40.290
[INAUDIBLE] position will
be from 0 to 1, right?

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:43.110
Time, and frame time.
[? So ?] [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:49.170
last clock values for the
last evaluation and 0 to 1

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:51.990
if the animation
is finished or not.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:55.480
And then the animation
configuration.

00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:58.980
So the target value
duration easing.

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:02.320
So you see, if you are familiar
to the vanilla animated

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:05.770
API from React Native, it's
a bit more complex to execute

00:24:05.770 --> 00:24:07.570
a simple timing function.

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:10.750
But this-- so the barrier
to entry is higher,

00:24:10.750 --> 00:24:14.330
but this is way more
powerful functions.

00:24:14.330 --> 00:24:19.810
So this is why I really want us
to look at the low level of how

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:21.370
these functions work.

00:24:21.370 --> 00:24:25.100
Because this will enable us,
down the road, to go much,

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:26.410
much further.

00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:29.690
Because these functions
are incredibly powerful.

00:24:29.690 --> 00:24:36.000
So let me copy paste
here, the function.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:41.410
So timing from React
Native Reanimated.

00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:43.350
And so the clock we
get as parameter.

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:44.360
Let's create the state.

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:49.060
So I'm going to create it here.

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:49.560
State.

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:55.050
So I'm going to assign an
animation value for each state.

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:03.740
Position, frame time, time.

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:04.790
And we have the config.

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:15.040
So toValue, we're
going to update it,

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:19.300
depending-- toValue is
if the position is 0,

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:23.500
the position is going to
be our starting value.

00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:28.540
If the starting value is 0,
the destination value is 1.

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:31.480
And if the position is
1, the destination value

00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:32.740
is going to be 0.

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:33.980
Duration, I don't know.

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:36.510
Let's put 3 seconds.

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:41.700
And easing we can put
whatever, I think.

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:42.420
In, out.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:56.950
And it does not like
the configuration.

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:03.340
So I have [? how ?] is
