00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:10.220
So here I need to
import these nodes.

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:26.380
So you see it loops nicely from
1 to 0 and now from 0 to 1.

00:28:26.380 --> 00:28:31.530
Now let's make the
animation interruptible.

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:35.130
So you see here, you might
be under the impression

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:37.420
that this is a lot
of boilerplate code

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:39.910
to write a simple
looping animation.

00:28:39.910 --> 00:28:43.860
There are utility animation
functions in Redash

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:48.150
that allows you to do these
in a few lines of code.

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:50.680
Because all this boilerplate
can be done for you.

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:54.540
But I find it to be extremely
important to understand

00:28:54.540 --> 00:29:00.330
how these clocks and
animation evaluation work.

00:29:00.330 --> 00:29:03.200
Because this can
really unlock the power

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:07.430
of complex declarative
gestures and animations

00:29:07.430 --> 00:29:09.720
as we run at 60 FPS.

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:15.010
So this is why here we are doing
all the boilerplate manually.

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:19.200
So here we have the state play
which becomes true or false.

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:20.880
And the first thing
I want to do is

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:23.850
to have an animation value
that matches the state of play.

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:28.050
So if play is true, I want
my animation value to be 1.

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:31.650
If play is false, my
animation value to be 0.

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:34.620
So here, we're going
to create an animation

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:37.590
value called isPlaying.

00:29:37.590 --> 00:29:39.380
So default value is 0.

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:41.080
[INAUDIBLE] default
state is false.

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:42.180
So that's good.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:43.920
And we're going
to create a hook.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:46.860
Again, a useCode hook.

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:50.130
That we set the
animation value isPlaying

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:53.280
to match the state
of the play variable.

00:29:56.300 --> 00:30:00.980
And you see here, I could
write it here, in useCode.

00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:05.030
But I'm having different
dependencies now.

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:06.130
So let me show you.

00:30:06.130 --> 00:30:08.420
So I do isPlaying.

00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:10.280
So if play is true it's 1.

00:30:10.280 --> 00:30:11.870
If not, 0.

00:30:11.870 --> 00:30:16.930
And here the
dependencies is play.

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:23.390
So when play changes,
this instruction changes.

00:30:23.390 --> 00:30:25.650
Because it depends
on the play variable.

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:30.050
And this is why I'm putting it
into a separate useCode block.

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:32.640
Because I don't want to have a
dependency on the play variable

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:33.800
here.

00:30:33.800 --> 00:30:36.770
What I'm going to write
the instructions--

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:40.010
I'm going to declare here are
valid for the whole lifetime

00:30:40.010 --> 00:30:41.150
of the component.

00:30:41.150 --> 00:30:43.370
So I don't want to
recreate these animation

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:49.980
nodes if the play variable
has changed, only isPlaying.

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:55.150
So now, we should have the
isPlaying variable that matches

00:30:55.150 --> 00:30:57.690
the state of the component.

00:30:57.690 --> 00:31:00.840
And so we're going to
[INAUDIBLE] actually.

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:04.340
So we're going to
write two conditions.

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:11.500
So the first condition is if
the state of the animation

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:15.650
is playing and the
clock is not running.

00:31:15.650 --> 00:31:21.180
So not clockRunning.

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:23.840
And here you see you have
to count the parentheses.

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:25.050
We want to start the clock.

00:31:25.050 --> 00:31:28.430
We want to start the animation.

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:30.350
And the other way around.

00:31:30.350 --> 00:31:42.050
If the animation is not playing,
and the clock is running,

00:31:42.050 --> 00:31:43.140
we want to stop the clock.

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:57.140
So I need to import
stopClock and [? the and. ?]

00:31:57.140 --> 00:31:57.890
Let's have a look.

00:32:03.690 --> 00:32:07.040
So [INAUDIBLE] play.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:09.740
And I can pause.

00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:10.670
Resume.

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:13.740
And you see, it doesn't
resume at the proper state.

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:15.290
But I can pause.

00:32:15.290 --> 00:32:17.030
And I can start the animation.

00:32:17.030 --> 00:32:18.550
But when I restart
the animation,

00:32:18.550 --> 00:32:23.300
the state of the animation is
screwed up, pardon my French.

00:32:27.140 --> 00:32:29.960
So here if the clock
is not running.

00:32:34.970 --> 00:32:37.120
So if we pause the
