in order to build complex
user interactions.

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:41.050
And we've seen how we don't
write imperative JavaScript

00:33:41.050 --> 00:33:43.680
code, but [INAUDIBLE]
animation nodes,

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:47.410
which are declarations
on the UI Thread.

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:52.470
And so all the primitives
we have in JavaScript, if,

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:55.360
and, not--

00:33:55.360 --> 00:34:00.180
we can-- the semicolon to
have sequential instructions.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:03.130
So we have the block here.

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:07.180
So we have an equivalent
for all the JavaScript

00:34:07.180 --> 00:34:10.660
constructs as animation nodes.

00:34:10.660 --> 00:34:13.680
And the only construct that is--

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:17.010
so you can have variable
assignments, sequentials,

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:19.160
executions, conditional nodes.

00:34:19.160 --> 00:34:24.660
The only primitive that is
not available are loops.

00:34:24.660 --> 00:34:26.180
But here-- but that's OK.

00:34:26.180 --> 00:34:32.570
Because here it's [? code ?] to
be evaluated for every frame.

00:34:32.570 --> 00:34:35.370
So I hope you
enjoyed this example.

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:38.150
If this makes sense to
you, then you really

00:34:38.150 --> 00:34:41.960
have unlocked the power
of declarative gestures

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:42.590
and animations.

00:34:42.590 --> 00:34:48.680
If you are able to switch
from the imperative mindset

00:34:48.680 --> 00:34:51.740
to the declarative
mindset, and understand

00:34:51.740 --> 00:34:55.430
how you can use clocks and
animation states in order

00:34:55.430 --> 00:34:58.690
to build different
user interactions,

00:34:58.690 --> 00:35:03.100
then the world of animations
and gestures in React Native

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:03.860
is your oyster.

00:35:03.860 --> 00:35:08.930
I think you can really go very
far with these primitives.

00:35:08.930 --> 00:35:11.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:35:14.420 --> 00:35:18.880
So we have looked at
transitions, animations.

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:21.500
Now let us play
around with gestures.

00:35:21.500 --> 00:35:24.720
We have a bunch of cards here.

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:28.910
And let's assign some gesture
handler to these cards.

00:35:28.910 --> 00:35:31.770
And play around,
see what we can do.

00:35:31.770 --> 00:35:36.110
And so here I am looping
over all my cards.

00:35:36.110 --> 00:35:39.380
And maybe what I
can do here is wrap

00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:47.160
a PanGestureHandler from
React Native Gesture Handler.

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:51.010
So we can maybe move
these cards around.

00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:55.780
And we need to pass two
properties for the gesture

00:35:55.780 --> 00:35:57.480
handler to work.

00:35:57.480 --> 00:36:01.840
And we're going to
use again [INAUDIBLE]..

00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:03.450
So it's quite some boilerplate.

00:36:03.450 --> 00:36:04.890
There is a lot of
animation values

00:36:04.890 --> 00:36:10.260
to be created [? to ?]
properties to be

00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:11.830
assigned in PanGestureHandler.

00:36:11.830 --> 00:36:16.990
So we are going to use utility
function, again from Redash

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:19.260
in order to have all this
boilerplate done for us.

00:36:19.260 --> 00:36:22.600
But you know, again,
don't be intimidated.

00:36:22.600 --> 00:36:25.410
You can look at the
Redash source code

00:36:25.410 --> 00:36:27.780
or at the react-native
gesture handler source code

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:33.540
to see what kind of boilerplate
is required for these gesture

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:35.560
handlers to work.

00:36:35.560 --> 00:36:37.980
But we're going to get the
gesture handler from a function

00:36:37.980 --> 00:36:42.190
called usePanGestureHandler.

00:36:42.190 --> 00:36:44.620
And it gives us a couple of
variables, a gesture handler

00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:47.150
that we can assign here.

00:36:47.150 --> 00:36:54.120
And the translation vector
of the card, the velocity.

00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:55.660
So when we release the gesture--

00:36:55.660 --> 00:36:57.460
[INAUDIBLE] velocity
of the gesture,

00:36:57.460 --> 00:36:58.720
and the state of the gesture.

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:00.910
Is the gesture active?

00:37:00.910 --> 00:37:03.250
Is the gesture [INAUDIBLE] and--

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:08.180
so here we assign the
PanGestureHandler.

00:37:08.180 --> 00:37:10.970
I probably need
to move key here.

00:37:10.970 --> 00:37:13.820
And the PanGestureHandler
takes a single [? children ?]

00:37:13.820 --> 00:37:17.030
which needs to be an
Animated.View from Reanimated.

00:37:19.610 --> 00:37:25.790
So here we use Animated.View
from react-native-reanimated.

00:37:25.790 --> 00:37:32.270
So we get the
translation vector here.

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:36.200
I think we can assign it
to this transformation

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:39.210
to see if we can
move the card around.

00:37:39.210 --> 00:37:42.300
So we're going to
use a transform.

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:47.620
And there is a utility function
in Redash called translate

00:37:47.620 --> 00:37:48.870
where we can give it a vector.

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:54.670
And it will apply
translate x, translate y.

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:56.560
A simple shortcut.

00:37:56.560 --> 00:38:01.660
So here I can move
the cards around.

00:38:01.660 --> 00:38:06.010
But I should create
one PanGestureHandler

00:38:06.010 --> 00:38:07.560
for each gesture handler.

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:08.980
So I'm just going
to move it here.

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:15.850
Ah-ha.

00:38:15.850 --> 00:38:18.220
So you see I can move the card.

00:38:18.220 --> 00:38:23.800
But now if I start
the gesture again,

00:38:23.800 --> 00:38:26.170
it starts from the
original position.

00:38:26.170 --> 00:38:29.440
So we need to save the
position in an offset value

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:31.360
when the gesture ends.

00:38:31.360 --> 00:38:37.540
And again in Redash, we have
a utility function for that.

00:38:37.540 --> 00:38:42.860
So I'm going to create
translate x equals withOffset.

00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:47.270
And the first parameter
is animation value.

00:38:47.270 --> 00:38:49.650
And the second parameter is
the state of the gesture.

00:38:49.650 --> 00:38:52.130
So that we know when
to save the offset.

00:38:52.130 --> 00:38:53.310
So translate x.

00:38:53.310 --> 00:38:54.890
We're going to do
the same for y.

00:38:59.650 --> 00:39:03.850
And here we're going
to apply the transform.

00:39:03.850 --> 00:39:07.280
So translate x.

00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:09.870
Translate y.

00:39:14.060 --> 00:39:15.230
It's translation.

00:39:20.470 --> 00:39:22.720
So here I can move
the card around.

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:29.440
And it remembers its
position across time.

00:39:29.440 --> 00:39:35.210
And you see, when I stop the
gesture, it's kind of abrupt.

00:39:35.210 --> 00:39:38.090
There is no like
physical momentum.

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:42.130
So what we can do here
instead of using withOffset,

00:39:42.130 --> 00:39:46.270
we can use withDecay, which
will add some nice decay.

00:39:46.270 --> 00:39:49.150
And this is where the
velocity comes into play.

00:39:49.150 --> 00:39:53.570
Because the decay is calculated
according to the velocity.

00:39:53.570 --> 00:39:57.090
So value is translation x.

00:39:57.090 --> 00:40:00.220
Velocity is velocity dot--

00:40:00.220 --> 00:40:01.640
oops.

00:40:01.640 --> 00:40:04.730
velocity.x.

00:40:04.730 --> 00:40:05.770
State is [? the ?] state.

00:40:08.340 --> 00:40:10.240
So same for y.

00:40:19.770 --> 00:40:27.600
So now you see you have
this nice momentum when

00:40:27.600 --> 00:40:29.310
moving the card.

00:40:29.310 --> 00:40:33.430
And you see it is
interruptable as well.

00:40:33.430 --> 00:40:40.790
So now let's have a nice wallet
animation using these concepts

00:40:40.790 --> 00:40:43.260
of PanGestureHandler and decay.

00:40:43.260 --> 00:40:47.360
So I'm going to move
this outside the loop.

00:40:50.010 --> 00:40:54.260
And we're going to move
the PanGestureHandler to be

00:40:54.260 --> 00:41:08.160
unique to [INAUDIBLE]
[? view. ?]

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:14.860
And we want to translate
only on the x-axis, actually.

00:41:14.860 --> 00:41:17.870
So we just want to scroll.

00:41:17.870 --> 00:41:18.930
Oops.

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:24.250
We just want to nicely
scroll [? our ?] cards.

00:41:24.250 --> 00:41:30.180
So something is not working.

00:41:30.180 --> 00:41:32.710
Because we forgot,
as I mentioned,

00:41:32.710 --> 00:41:36.860
the single child needs to
be an animated [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:40.670
But what I'm going
to do, because I

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:47.560
want to keep this one, I think
I'm going to wrap it here.

00:41:51.320 --> 00:41:53.320
And put an Animated.View.

00:42:00.460 --> 00:42:03.780
Let's see how it looks.

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:10.270
[LAUGHING] It's not
translate x, but translate y.

00:42:10.270 --> 00:42:10.770
OK.

00:42:18.600 --> 00:42:20.760
So it translates nicely.

00:42:20.760 --> 00:42:24.360
And we have the nice
momentum effect from decay.

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:28.210
So now we want to
clamp the values.

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:31.140
We're going to use a
function called diffClamp.

00:42:31.140 --> 00:42:35.130
So it clamps-- diffClamp
clamps of value with a min max.

00:42:35.130 --> 00:42:39.150
But we call it diffClamp,
meaning that, you know,

00:42:39.150 --> 00:42:41.790
if we go to minus
[? 100 ?] pixels,

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:45.580
just a delta of one pixel
will be taken into account.

00:42:45.580 --> 00:42:50.480
So it's much more
responsive if you want--

00:42:50.480 --> 00:42:54.470
if you scrolled far back in
one of the lower or upper bound

00:42:54.470 --> 00:42:56.180
and want to go back
to one of the--

00:42:58.690 --> 00:43:02.650
within the bounds of the
[INAUDIBLE] clamping.

00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:08.510
So we're going to
add diffClamp here.

00:43:08.510 --> 00:43:10.560
And here there is a trick.

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:15.460
So there's currently a bug in
the diffClamp implementation

00:43:15.460 --> 00:43:16.270
of Reanimated.

00:43:16.270 --> 00:43:20.180
So we're going to use
diffClamp from Redash.

00:43:20.180 --> 00:43:22.070
So in order to
not have this bug.

00:43:22.070 --> 00:43:26.450
So the maximum value is 0.

00:43:26.450 --> 00:43:26.950
Right.

00:43:26.950 --> 00:43:34.320
When we are at this state here.

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:39.060
And the minimum value
is going to be so--

00:43:42.550 --> 00:43:47.340
the length-- so the
height of all these cards

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:51.790
minus this height here.

00:43:51.790 --> 00:44:06.480
So this would be minus
cards.length times card height,

00:44:06.480 --> 00:44:10.050
which we have defined
here, perfect--

00:44:10.050 --> 00:44:12.360
minus the container
height, which

00:44:12.360 --> 00:44:15.010
we calculate using onLayout.

00:44:17.970 --> 00:44:19.340
So let's have a look.

00:44:19.340 --> 00:44:22.650
So here, so you see this is
where diffClamp comes in.

00:44:22.650 --> 00:44:24.690
If I use only
clamps, so here I'm

00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:27.510
scrolling, scrolling,
scrolling, scrolling, scrolling.

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:29.430
Then if I want to
scroll back like this,

00:44:29.430 --> 00:44:32.190
I would have to do all the
opposite scrolling that I did.

00:44:32.190 --> 00:44:35.370
But even [INAUDIBLE] maybe
I'm like 500 pixels right now.

00:44:35.370 --> 00:44:38.000
I can just immediately
go back up.

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:40.580
And this is what diffClamp does.

00:44:40.580 --> 00:44:42.330
I mean, I can show you
quickly with clamp.

00:44:44.460 --> 00:44:47.090
So I'm here I'm scrolling down,
scrolling down, scrolling down,

00:44:47.090 --> 00:44:48.290
scrolling down, scrolling down.

00:44:48.290 --> 00:44:49.790
If I want to scroll
up again, I need

00:44:49.790 --> 00:44:52.190
to scroll up, scroll up, scroll
up, scroll up, scroll up.

00:44:52.190 --> 00:44:53.380
And now it scrolls up.

00:44:53.380 --> 00:44:56.800
With diffClamps, it's
going to be automatic.

00:44:56.800 --> 00:44:58.390
Scroll down, scroll
down, scroll down.

00:44:58.390 --> 00:45:01.340
But you see
immediately scroll up.

00:45:01.340 --> 00:45:04.070
And let's look.

00:45:04.070 --> 00:45:07.860
So the lower bound
is not correct.

00:45:07.860 --> 00:45:09.260
It's plus container height.

00:45:09.260 --> 00:45:11.210
Sorry.

00:45:11.210 --> 00:45:16.350
So 0 and perfect.

00:45:16.350 --> 00:45:18.600
And very cool.

00:45:21.780 --> 00:45:28.570
So now let's animate our cards.

00:45:28.570 --> 00:45:33.220
So maybe we want to add--

00:45:36.080 --> 00:45:39.520
so we want, actually, now--

00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:41.020
we're going to clamp.

00:45:41.020 --> 00:45:43.450
So I'm going to call this y.

00:45:43.450 --> 00:45:45.100
So if the card has
reached the top,

00:45:45.100 --> 00:45:47.290
I don't want it to go above.

00:45:47.290 --> 00:45:51.200
I want to clamp its value.

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:56.680
So translate y.

00:45:56.680 --> 00:45:59.080
We're going to interpolate
from the y animation value.

00:46:06.960 --> 00:46:18.830
So the input range goes from
0 and if we translate to--

00:46:18.830 --> 00:46:21.530
so for the card to be at
the top, if it's 0, it's 0.

00:46:21.530 --> 00:46:31.940
If it's-- minus card width,
so that would be minus card--

00:46:31.940 --> 00:46:33.200
so card height, sorry--

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:35.790
times index.

00:46:35.790 --> 00:46:38.850
So this would be
our clamping value.

00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:44.140
So the output range
would be the same value.

00:46:44.140 --> 00:46:49.520
But we're going to use
extrapolate clamp, meaning--

00:46:49.520 --> 00:46:53.130
so this should be the maximum
value the card can go.

00:46:53.130 --> 00:46:56.200
So do not go outside the screen.

00:46:56.200 --> 00:46:57.310
And so by using--

00:46:57.310 --> 00:46:59.580
so here we use linear
interpolation. [? These ?]

00:46:59.580 --> 00:47:01.710
values match one to one.

00:47:01.710 --> 00:47:05.220
But when we have a
value, for instance,

00:47:05.220 --> 00:47:08.220
that is lower than minus
card height times index,

00:47:08.220 --> 00:47:12.300
we want to be at minus
card height times

00:47:12.300 --> 00:47:14.200
index, not a lower value.

00:47:14.200 --> 00:47:17.490
So we are going to
write extrapolate--

00:47:17.490 --> 00:47:18.870
extrapolate.clamp.

00:47:21.730 --> 00:47:23.570
Let's have a look.

00:47:23.570 --> 00:47:26.490
So we see the card moves
on top of each other.

00:47:31.930 --> 00:47:34.600
You can even add,
maybe, just, you know,

00:47:34.600 --> 00:47:38.470
if you want to build
some effects, here.

00:47:38.470 --> 00:47:41.170
Maybe it's minus
card height plus 16.

00:47:44.110 --> 00:47:45.300
Or minus 16, sorry.

00:47:48.600 --> 00:47:51.720
You [? see here-- ?]
24 [INAUDIBLE]..

00:47:51.720 --> 00:47:58.570
So you already have here
a nice wallet animation.

00:47:58.570 --> 00:48:04.260
So now we want to calculate
the position of each card.

00:48:06.910 --> 00:48:24.890
And so the position y is y plus
the index times card height.

00:48:24.890 --> 00:48:27.530
And we're going
to use this value

00:48:27.530 --> 00:48:31.610
to do a couple of
interpolations.

00:48:31.610 --> 00:48:42.550
So if the card is
disappearing, its position

00:48:42.550 --> 00:48:47.520
is at least minus height.

00:48:47.520 --> 00:48:51.100
So from minus 0 to minus height.

00:48:51.100 --> 00:48:51.850
Minus card height.

00:48:51.850 --> 00:48:53.970
Sorry.

00:48:53.970 --> 00:48:55.200
Right.

00:48:55.200 --> 00:48:59.400
So here, the card,
its position is 0.

00:49:03.310 --> 00:49:09.980
So if the card is on
top, its position is 0.

00:49:09.980 --> 00:49:13.680
If the card is at
the bottom here--

00:49:13.680 --> 00:49:16.520
so what is the
position on bottom?

00:49:19.220 --> 00:49:27.380
It's the number of visible
cards in the screen

00:49:27.380 --> 00:49:31.720
times the height of the card.

00:49:31.720 --> 00:49:38.320
So that would be visible
cards times card height.

00:49:38.320 --> 00:49:42.160
What are the number
of visible cards?

00:49:46.490 --> 00:49:53.170
So visible cards-- we do a
math floor of the container

00:49:53.170 --> 00:49:57.710
height divided by card height.

00:50:00.660 --> 00:50:02.680
And so this is the
number of visible cards.

00:50:05.750 --> 00:50:11.090
So this is-- so
minus 1 for the range

00:50:11.090 --> 00:50:15.790
and is appearing is the number
of visible cards times card

00:50:15.790 --> 00:50:17.720
height.

00:50:17.720 --> 00:50:26.660
So is bottom, is appearing.

00:50:31.040 --> 00:50:37.040
So now we can create some
scale transformation.

00:50:41.530 --> 00:50:43.720
So the scale of the card--

00:50:43.720 --> 00:50:46.420
so we interpolate
from position y.

00:50:49.450 --> 00:50:58.540
So the input range is
disappearing is on top,

00:50:58.540 --> 00:51:08.310
is on bottom, and is appearing.

00:51:08.310 --> 00:51:11.850
And the animation values are--

00:51:11.850 --> 00:51:14.580
so 0, 5, it's disappearing.

00:51:14.580 --> 00:51:18.270
1, 1 if it's in
the visible range.

00:51:18.270 --> 00:51:20.230
And 0, 5, when it's appearing.

00:51:20.230 --> 00:51:22.740
Let's try that.

00:51:22.740 --> 00:51:25.950
So here you see it disappears
nicely in the background.

00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:29.220
And it appears nicely here.

00:51:29.220 --> 00:51:30.850
What we can do is
add some clamping.

00:51:37.970 --> 00:51:39.910
That looks good.

00:51:39.910 --> 00:51:42.030
Let's add some opacity.

00:51:42.030 --> 00:51:44.450
So same story for opacity.

00:51:49.710 --> 00:51:51.360
So I can just add it here.

00:51:55.440 --> 00:52:04.770
And here, 1-- oh,
actually, the same value.

00:52:04.770 --> 00:52:05.270
No.

00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:17.170
What we want to do here
is to not clamp, I think.

00:52:17.170 --> 00:52:24.490
So we see here it
disappears nicely.

00:52:24.490 --> 00:52:32.020
And so one thing we can fix is,
you see when the card appears,

00:52:32.020 --> 00:52:35.920
it's too far away
from the top card.

00:52:35.920 --> 00:52:39.110
It's not super smooth.

00:52:39.110 --> 00:52:42.490
So we can add some
extra translation here.

00:52:45.010 --> 00:52:49.700
So here we can add.

00:52:49.700 --> 00:52:51.380
So you see here,
we don't do a plus.

00:52:51.380 --> 00:52:54.950
Again, we use [INAUDIBLE]
[? add ?] animation node.

00:52:54.950 --> 00:53:03.260
So let's call it
extra translation y.

00:53:03.260 --> 00:53:08.000
So which we're going to
interpolate from position y.

00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:12.920
So actually let
me move these up.

00:53:12.920 --> 00:53:18.770
So we interpolate
from position y.

00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:24.040
Oops.

00:53:24.040 --> 00:53:26.000
So input range.

00:53:26.000 --> 00:53:33.230
So we go from isOnBottom
on bottom to isAppearing.

00:53:36.040 --> 00:53:36.860
Output range.

00:53:36.860 --> 00:53:39.320
So at bottom, the
translation is 0.

00:53:39.320 --> 00:53:47.750
At isAppearing, we know that
at isAppearing, the size is--

00:53:47.750 --> 00:53:49.010
scale 0, 5.

00:53:49.010 --> 00:53:52.910
So the height is card
height divided by 2.

00:53:52.910 --> 00:53:56.370
And we want to move it by
half of the card height.

00:53:56.370 --> 00:53:59.810
So it's going to be card
height divided by 4.

00:53:59.810 --> 00:54:01.850
And of course, here
we want to clamp.

00:54:01.850 --> 00:54:07.580
So we don't want to go
outside these values.

00:54:07.580 --> 00:54:10.040
Extrapolate clamp.

00:54:10.040 --> 00:54:10.880
Let's have a look.

00:54:13.340 --> 00:54:13.840
No.

00:54:13.840 --> 00:54:14.470
It's not.

00:54:14.470 --> 00:54:20.700
It's minus card
height divided by 4.

00:54:20.700 --> 00:54:26.590
And you see now it
looks very cool.

00:54:26.590 --> 00:54:31.610
And one thing we can
do here [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:31.610 --> 00:54:38.020
the scroll is not
completely over.

00:54:38.020 --> 00:54:41.930
So we don't want to
stop the scroll on--

00:54:41.930 --> 00:54:43.190
I'm being very picky here.

00:54:43.190 --> 00:54:46.760
But it doesn't feel
nice that the end scroll

00:54:46.760 --> 00:54:48.520
position is this.

00:54:48.520 --> 00:54:57.230
So the last card should be at
least as the full position.

00:54:57.230 --> 00:55:07.100
So here, maybe
instead of container,

00:55:07.100 --> 00:55:08.930
we can do visible cards.

00:55:12.980 --> 00:55:16.720
Now what do we need to do?

00:55:16.720 --> 00:55:18.250
So we container height.

00:55:22.710 --> 00:55:28.350
Let's try visible cards
times card height.

00:55:28.350 --> 00:55:29.960
Yes.

00:55:29.960 --> 00:55:30.920
So that looks good.

00:55:30.920 --> 00:55:36.490
So here our end scroll
position looks much nicer.

00:55:36.490 --> 00:55:41.340
So you see a pretty
cool user interaction

00:55:41.340 --> 00:55:46.040
using the PanGestureHandler from
react-native GestureHandler.

00:55:46.040 --> 00:55:50.970
But there of course are tons
of different user interactions

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:54.430
you can build using these APIs.

00:55:54.430 --> 00:55:58.260
But I hope that this nice
[INAUDIBLE] user interactions

00:55:58.260 --> 00:56:03.530
really gave you some inspiration
on the kind of gestures

00:56:03.530 --> 00:56:06.570
and animations you can
build using these APIs.

00:56:06.570 --> 00:56:09.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:56:12.400 --> 00:56:15.710
These gestures and
animations we just looked at

00:56:15.710 --> 00:56:19.010
integrate seamlessly
with react-native-svg.

00:56:19.010 --> 00:56:22.400
And this allows for
an interesting range

00:56:22.400 --> 00:56:27.740
of fun and creative user
experiences and components.

00:56:27.740 --> 00:56:31.400
And this is thanks to
an incredible community

00:56:31.400 --> 00:56:33.120
and open source work.

00:56:33.120 --> 00:56:33.620
Right?

00:56:33.620 --> 00:56:35.690
So we looked at react-native
gesture handler,

00:56:35.690 --> 00:56:38.390
react-native-reanimated,
which allows

00:56:38.390 --> 00:56:42.740
us to declare our
animations on the UI Thread.

00:56:42.740 --> 00:56:45.710
And then there has been
a tremendous amount

00:56:45.710 --> 00:56:47.900
of open source and
community work which

00:56:47.900 --> 00:56:50.600
has been spent in order
for these libraries

00:56:50.600 --> 00:56:55.280
to nicely integrate
with react-native-svg.

00:56:55.280 --> 00:56:58.730
And so I would like to show
you a simple example, which is

00:56:58.730 --> 00:57:02.060
going to be a circular slider.

00:57:02.060 --> 00:57:06.290
So here we have a svg circle.

00:57:06.290 --> 00:57:08.360
Here the circular progress.

00:57:08.360 --> 00:57:13.870
And so we have the
white one here.

00:57:13.870 --> 00:57:19.880
And we're going to animate
the stroke of this one

00:57:19.880 --> 00:57:23.080
to show some progress.

00:57:23.080 --> 00:57:27.100
And here we have a
cursor component,

00:57:27.100 --> 00:57:31.510
which we are going to
be able to move around.

00:57:31.510 --> 00:57:37.370
And its value will always
fit the bounds of the circle.

00:57:37.370 --> 00:57:41.050
So here we have a
theta animation value.

00:57:41.050 --> 00:57:43.680
And we are going to
move the cursor around

00:57:43.680 --> 00:57:44.680
to update this value.

00:57:44.680 --> 00:57:46.510
So theta goes from 0.

00:57:46.510 --> 00:57:48.450
This is the position at 0.

00:57:48.450 --> 00:57:52.660
Or actually, here we have a
rotate of minus 90 degrees.

00:57:52.660 --> 00:57:55.220
So 0 is going to be here.

00:57:55.220 --> 00:57:57.670
Let me remove this one for now.

00:57:57.670 --> 00:57:59.830
So we're going to have 0 here.

00:57:59.830 --> 00:58:05.050
We move pi minus pi 0.

00:58:05.050 --> 00:58:06.860
And so maybe here we're
going to normalize.

00:58:06.860 --> 00:58:12.190
Because this goes from
0 to pi minus pi 0.

00:58:12.190 --> 00:58:15.280
We're going to normalize
it to go from 0 to 2 pi.

00:58:15.280 --> 00:58:18.190
360 degrees.

00:58:18.190 --> 00:58:22.610
And the way we're
going to do it--

00:58:22.610 --> 00:58:24.700
we are going to set--

00:58:24.700 --> 00:58:27.190
so we have a theta value, 0.

00:58:27.190 --> 00:58:31.900
This will set the position of
the cursor on to the circle.

00:58:31.900 --> 00:58:33.400
How are we going to do it?

00:58:33.400 --> 00:58:36.730
Well, we have three
coordinate systems

00:58:36.730 --> 00:58:38.410
to work with in react-native.

00:58:38.410 --> 00:58:40.420
There is a [INAUDIBLE]
coordinate system.

00:58:40.420 --> 00:58:43.960
So there is an x-axis which
goes from the left to right,

00:58:43.960 --> 00:58:48.250
and the y-axis, which
goes from top to bottom.

00:58:48.250 --> 00:58:50.580
This is [INAUDIBLE]
coordinate system.

00:58:50.580 --> 00:58:53.650
Then there is a Cartesian
coordinate system,

00:58:53.650 --> 00:58:58.000
where we have a center
of origin somewhere.

00:58:58.000 --> 00:59:02.300
Here our center is going to
be the middle of the circle.

00:59:02.300 --> 00:59:08.340
And the y-axis goes from
0 from bottom to top.

00:59:08.340 --> 00:59:09.760
So the opposite
of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:09.760 --> 00:59:11.810
axis, which goes
from top to bottom.

00:59:11.810 --> 00:59:14.920
And then we have the
x-axis, which is identical.

00:59:14.920 --> 00:59:16.900
It goes from left to right.

00:59:16.900 --> 00:59:19.380
This is our Cartesian
coordinate system.

00:59:19.380 --> 00:59:23.660
The third coordinate system
is a polar coordinate system.

00:59:23.660 --> 00:59:26.620
So here we also have an origin.

00:59:26.620 --> 00:59:33.820
And given an x and y value
in the Cartesian coordinate

00:59:33.820 --> 00:59:38.680
system, we are going to get
an angle in a theta value

00:59:38.680 --> 00:59:41.960
in radians and a radius.

00:59:41.960 --> 00:59:46.460
So we take the angle.

00:59:46.460 --> 00:59:50.100
We convert it to the-- so
we have a polar coordinate.

00:59:50.100 --> 00:59:52.260
So we have a radius
and an angle.

00:59:52.260 --> 00:59:56.090
We can convert it to a
[INAUDIBLE] coordinate,

00:59:56.090 --> 00:59:58.480
which would give us x and
y positions [INAUDIBLE]..

00:59:58.480 --> 01:00:01.300
So that will position the
cursor on to the circle.

01:00:01.300 --> 01:00:03.190
Then we move the circle around.

01:00:03.190 --> 01:00:05.020
So we have an x and
y-coordinate which

01:00:05.020 --> 01:00:07.360
we can transform to
a polar coordinate

01:00:07.360 --> 01:00:09.610
in order to know
the angle theta.

01:00:09.610 --> 01:00:12.070
And so we can assign
the theta value,

01:00:12.070 --> 01:00:19.020
which will drive the
animations of the stroke value.

01:00:19.020 --> 01:00:22.650
And also maybe we can animate
the color of the progress.

01:00:22.650 --> 01:00:26.920
So maybe you want green at
the beginning, red at the end.

01:00:26.920 --> 01:00:29.910
And so if you go on
my YouTube channel,

01:00:29.910 --> 01:00:32.250
I will put the links in
the video description.

01:00:32.250 --> 01:00:36.570
I have videos on how we can
convert from one coordinate

01:00:36.570 --> 01:00:37.980
system to the other.

01:00:37.980 --> 01:00:42.540
Here we're going to use again
utility functions from Redash

01:00:42.540 --> 01:00:46.920
to seamlessly go from
one to the other.

01:00:46.920 --> 01:00:49.300
So let's get started.

01:00:49.300 --> 01:00:52.050
So we are going to
start with our cursor.

01:00:52.050 --> 01:00:54.780
And once we have--

01:00:54.780 --> 01:00:58.410
so the cursor is going to
[? write ?] the data value.

01:00:58.410 --> 01:01:02.790
And once we have it working,
we will look into animate

01:01:02.790 --> 01:01:06.310
the circular progress
component that we have here.

01:01:06.310 --> 01:01:08.920
So let's look at the cursor.

01:01:08.920 --> 01:01:10.500
So the first thing
we're going to do

01:01:10.500 --> 01:01:15.870
is to wrap a PanGestureHandler
so we can move it around.

01:01:15.870 --> 01:01:22.050
So PanGestureHandler from
react-native gesture handler.

01:01:22.050 --> 01:01:25.060
And we need the gesture handler.

01:01:25.060 --> 01:01:29.740
We're going to use the
helper function from Redash.

01:01:29.740 --> 01:01:34.320
So we have gesture handler.

01:01:34.320 --> 01:01:35.640
usePanGestureHandler.

01:01:38.190 --> 01:01:42.930
And we're going to get
a translation vector

01:01:42.930 --> 01:01:47.580
and the state of the gesture.

01:01:47.580 --> 01:01:48.820
Oops.

01:01:48.820 --> 01:01:49.700
Some [? typo ?] here.

01:01:52.680 --> 01:01:58.900
And so here we're going
to add some translate.

01:02:01.660 --> 01:02:05.090
But what-- so what
we want to do--

01:02:05.090 --> 01:02:05.590
OK.

01:02:05.590 --> 01:02:06.630
Let's add some translate.

01:02:06.630 --> 01:02:08.560
So translate x,
we're going to use--

01:02:08.560 --> 01:02:10.780
so we want to
remember the position

01:02:10.780 --> 01:02:12.680
across different gestures.

01:02:12.680 --> 01:02:15.680
So we're going to
use withOffset.

01:02:15.680 --> 01:02:20.630
So translate x state
and translate y.

01:02:24.400 --> 01:02:27.450
Translate y.

01:02:27.450 --> 01:02:29.930
[INAUDIBLE] translation.

01:02:29.930 --> 01:02:32.860
Let me apply the transformation.

01:02:37.710 --> 01:02:40.930
Translate x.

01:02:40.930 --> 01:02:44.010
Translate y.

01:02:44.010 --> 01:02:46.460
So I can move the cursor around.

01:02:46.460 --> 01:02:46.960
Super.

01:02:46.960 --> 01:02:48.960
But now it needs
to be, you know,

01:02:48.960 --> 01:02:53.170
whatever is the position within
the bounds of the circle.

01:02:53.170 --> 01:02:57.240
So this is where we need
to convert these values

01:02:57.240 --> 01:03:01.420
into polar coordinates.

01:03:01.420 --> 01:03:08.350
So I'm going to create x, y.

01:03:08.350 --> 01:03:12.510
And we have a
center vector, which

01:03:12.510 --> 01:03:16.690
is the middle of the screen.

01:03:16.690 --> 01:03:21.780
So let me create a vector.

01:03:21.780 --> 01:03:30.520
So center is x is width
divided by 2. y is height

01:03:30.520 --> 01:03:38.590
divided by 2, which we need to
import from the dimensions API.

01:03:38.590 --> 01:03:46.140
So we have width, height
from dimension get window.

01:03:51.290 --> 01:03:54.740
So let's convert these
into polar coordinates.

01:03:57.530 --> 01:03:59.570
So we have polar equals--

01:03:59.570 --> 01:04:01.120
so these are Cartesian.

01:04:01.120 --> 01:04:06.000
So we're going to use
Cartesian to polar.

01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:08.660
The math behind this function
is super interesting.

01:04:08.660 --> 01:04:11.110
I have some videos
on this topic.

01:04:11.110 --> 01:04:12.840
So if you're
interested, I definitely

01:04:12.840 --> 01:04:14.340
recommend you check it out.

01:04:14.340 --> 01:04:15.800
The first argument is a point.

01:04:15.800 --> 01:04:18.660
So we have x, y.

01:04:18.660 --> 01:04:25.320
And we probably need
to [INAUDIBLE] polar--

01:04:25.320 --> 01:04:26.530
no, Cartesian to polar.

01:04:26.530 --> 01:04:29.230
That's correct.

01:04:29.230 --> 01:04:35.860
And so we get the theta
angle, which we can

01:04:35.860 --> 01:04:38.940
assign to this animation value.

01:04:38.940 --> 01:04:41.600
The radius-- here the
radius doesn't matter.

01:04:41.600 --> 01:04:45.480
What we need to do
is to set the radius

01:04:45.480 --> 01:04:50.770
to be the radius of our
circle and convert back

01:04:50.770 --> 01:04:56.480
these coordinates to
Cartesian coordinates.

01:04:56.480 --> 01:05:04.900
So we are going to have x
translate x, y translate y.

01:05:04.900 --> 01:05:09.400
So we are going to convert
polar to Cartesian.

01:05:12.570 --> 01:05:15.240
Theta is polar.theta.

01:05:15.240 --> 01:05:15.740
theta.

01:05:15.740 --> 01:05:16.460
No problem.

01:05:16.460 --> 01:05:26.780
But radius is not the
radius of our cursor,

01:05:26.780 --> 01:05:28.280
but the radius of the circle.

01:05:28.280 --> 01:05:32.940
So that will bound the cursor
to only move around the circle.

01:05:32.940 --> 01:05:38.480
And the second
parameter should be

01:05:38.480 --> 01:05:43.140
polar to coordinate
to Cartesian.

01:05:43.140 --> 01:05:44.240
It looks good.

01:05:47.160 --> 01:05:51.000
Is this radius--
radius [INAUDIBLE]..

01:05:51.000 --> 01:05:56.500
So you see we have an issue.

01:05:59.050 --> 01:06:02.600
So translate x with the center.

01:06:02.600 --> 01:06:09.780
The origin of-- so I think
I should set center here.

01:06:14.080 --> 01:06:14.600
Sorry.

01:06:14.600 --> 01:06:16.890
OK.

01:06:16.890 --> 01:06:18.240
It's not Cartesian to polar.

01:06:18.240 --> 01:06:19.980
But [INAUDIBLE] to polar.

01:06:19.980 --> 01:06:21.780
My mistake.

01:06:21.780 --> 01:06:24.150
So [INAUDIBLE] to polar.

01:06:24.150 --> 01:06:27.320
And we can set the center
[? of ?] origin here.

01:06:27.320 --> 01:06:28.500
So the center.

01:06:28.500 --> 01:06:34.600
And here it's convert
to polar to [INAUDIBLE]..

01:06:34.600 --> 01:06:37.090
I was confused
why it didn't need

01:06:37.090 --> 01:06:41.560
to use the center of origin--

01:06:41.560 --> 01:06:46.810
the origin-- that
didn't make any sense.

01:06:46.810 --> 01:06:52.370
So now still actually,
it doesn't work.

01:06:52.370 --> 01:06:53.160
We have a weird--

01:06:57.590 --> 01:07:05.010
so actually here, it's the
center because it's not--

01:07:09.790 --> 01:07:13.710
the container, so
it's not the window,

01:07:13.710 --> 01:07:18.780
but the content, which is the
size here of this container,

01:07:18.780 --> 01:07:24.570
of the circle [INAUDIBLE]
I put background color red.

01:07:24.570 --> 01:07:26.680
So you see our
center is not height

01:07:26.680 --> 01:07:32.860
of the screen divided by 2,
but radius and the radius.

01:07:32.860 --> 01:07:38.230
So let me update this.

01:07:38.230 --> 01:07:43.740
So center is radius.

01:07:50.110 --> 01:08:03.230
And so now it animates nicely.

01:08:03.230 --> 01:08:07.100
So now we need to
set the theta value.

01:08:07.100 --> 01:08:12.730
And so you could set the value
to be polar.theta directly.

01:08:12.730 --> 01:08:20.440
But polar.theta goes from 0
to pi and minus pi to minus 0.

01:08:20.440 --> 01:08:24.230
So here the value would
be minus pi divided by 2.

01:08:24.230 --> 01:08:27.570
And not 3/4 of 2 pi.

01:08:27.570 --> 01:08:32.570
So we want to normalize the
value to go from 0 to 2 pi.

01:08:32.570 --> 01:08:36.470
So we're going to
create useCode and we're

01:08:36.470 --> 01:08:42.590
going to assign
[INAUDIBLE] and we're

01:08:42.590 --> 01:08:48.630
going to assign theta
to be polar dot theta.

01:08:48.630 --> 01:08:50.460
But we need to normalize theta.

01:08:56.760 --> 01:09:00.400
So normalize theta.

01:09:00.400 --> 01:09:04.110
Or we take theta--

01:09:04.110 --> 01:09:07.470
so as an animated node.

01:09:07.470 --> 01:09:19.570
So if theta is less than 0,
so it's minus 0 minus pi,

01:09:19.570 --> 01:09:21.730
we need to add 2 pi.

01:09:21.730 --> 01:09:25.270
So theta is less than 0.

01:09:25.270 --> 01:09:29.830
It's theta plus 2 pi.

01:09:33.020 --> 01:09:35.160
If not, it's the theta value.

01:09:35.160 --> 01:09:38.960
So from 0 to pi theta is OK.

01:09:38.960 --> 01:09:47.450
And now if let's say we have
minus pi minus pi plus 2 pi,

01:09:47.450 --> 01:09:55.050
would be 2 pi 3/4 of 2 pi.

01:09:55.050 --> 01:09:59.690
So it's going to be 2
pi minus half of 1 pi.

01:09:59.690 --> 01:10:06.150
So this should give us
proper values for theta.

01:10:06.150 --> 01:10:08.480
And here, let's see.

01:10:08.480 --> 01:10:10.610
We're going to interpolate
the [? color ?] on theta

01:10:10.610 --> 01:10:13.080
to see what we get.

01:10:13.080 --> 01:10:15.880
So let's use interpolate.

01:10:15.880 --> 01:10:18.260
So colors are numbers.

01:10:18.260 --> 01:10:21.880
And we have a interpolate
color function in Redash.

01:10:21.880 --> 01:10:25.540
So we can interpolate on theta.

01:10:25.540 --> 01:10:27.880
And so input range--

01:10:27.880 --> 01:10:31.340
so theta goes from 0 to 2 pi.

01:10:31.340 --> 01:10:32.470
So we have 0.

01:10:32.470 --> 01:10:35.890
Let's say pi and 2 pi.

01:10:35.890 --> 01:10:37.810
We're going to use three colors.

01:10:37.810 --> 01:10:51.020
Output range-- we can use style
guide, palette, secondary,

01:10:51.020 --> 01:10:55.140
primary, and primary.

01:10:59.420 --> 01:11:03.360
And tertiary.

01:11:03.360 --> 01:11:06.230
Let's have a look.

01:11:06.230 --> 01:11:11.320
So we see here the
color updates nicely.

01:11:11.320 --> 01:11:18.770
So now let's animate
our svg circle.

01:11:18.770 --> 01:11:26.260
And we're going to use a
dash stroke array to animate

01:11:26.260 --> 01:11:28.580
the progress of the circle.

01:11:28.580 --> 01:11:32.650
So we want dash stroke array to
be circumference of the circle.

01:11:32.650 --> 01:11:37.600
So we want one empty stroke
to be the circumference

01:11:37.600 --> 01:11:38.500
of the circle.

01:11:38.500 --> 01:11:42.730
One full stroke to be the
circumference of the circle--

01:11:42.730 --> 01:11:44.380
so we can animate,
really, from 0

01:11:44.380 --> 01:11:49.120
completely empty to
1 completely full.

01:11:49.120 --> 01:11:50.890
So the circumference
of the circle

01:11:50.890 --> 01:11:57.890
is 2 pi times the radius.

01:11:57.890 --> 01:12:02.380
And the length of
the arc of circle

01:12:02.380 --> 01:12:07.900
is going to be radius
times the theta, right?

01:12:07.900 --> 01:12:11.050
And so this is going
to be the offset--

01:12:11.050 --> 01:12:13.360
the dash stroke offset.

01:12:13.360 --> 01:12:19.400
So let's-- so we need to
calculate the circumference.

01:12:22.980 --> 01:12:29.380
Which is going to be
2 pi times the radius.

01:12:32.870 --> 01:12:37.290
Which we get--
[INAUDIBLE] as property.

01:12:37.290 --> 01:12:43.390
So we can assign dash
stroke array to be--

01:12:46.050 --> 01:12:50.830
so circumference, circumference.

01:12:50.830 --> 01:12:55.760
And we can calculate the--

01:12:55.760 --> 01:12:57.040
so here, OK.

01:12:57.040 --> 01:12:59.800
So it's dash stroke offset.

01:12:59.800 --> 01:13:01.300
And here-- so
we're going to have

01:13:01.300 --> 01:13:02.580
a couple of animation values.

01:13:02.580 --> 01:13:06.150
It's not a circle, but
an animated circle.

01:13:06.150 --> 01:13:09.220
An animated circle.

01:13:09.220 --> 01:13:11.510
We use create
animated components

01:13:11.510 --> 01:13:14.740
in order to have the animated
wrapper that can accept

01:13:14.740 --> 01:13:17.890
animation values as property.

01:13:17.890 --> 01:13:21.730
So now we need to calculate
stroke dash offset.

01:13:24.580 --> 01:13:27.520
Which is actually the same
formula [INAUDIBLE] here.

01:13:27.520 --> 01:13:31.570
But instead of having the
full angle, which is 2 pi,

01:13:31.570 --> 01:13:33.520
we use theta.

01:13:33.520 --> 01:13:39.720
So it's going to be
multiply theta by radius.

01:13:39.720 --> 01:13:42.570
Let's have a look.

01:13:42.570 --> 01:13:43.620
Looks good.

01:13:43.620 --> 01:13:48.420
But also very strange.

01:13:48.420 --> 01:13:57.820
So the stroke color
appears to be good.

01:14:01.190 --> 01:14:02.000
Background color.

01:14:17.100 --> 01:14:20.750
We need to add stroke width.

01:14:25.500 --> 01:14:29.190
And now there is a
strange offset value here.

01:14:32.000 --> 01:14:36.680
So here it looks like
at 0, it's correct.

01:14:36.680 --> 01:14:39.420
And then there is-- you
see some offset value

01:14:39.420 --> 01:14:42.920
that goes further and further.

01:14:47.330 --> 01:14:49.750
And I think here I
wrote it down because I

01:14:49.750 --> 01:14:57.110
wrote radius is the radius minus
the stroke width divided by 2.

01:14:57.110 --> 01:14:58.280
That makes sense.

01:14:58.280 --> 01:15:01.070
Because we want the
center to be here.

01:15:01.070 --> 01:15:06.970
So I should probably
replace radius here.

01:15:06.970 --> 01:15:07.470
Yeah.

01:15:07.470 --> 01:15:08.700
Now that looks good.

01:15:12.670 --> 01:15:15.520
It's fun, isn't it?

01:15:15.520 --> 01:15:18.220
I could play with this
always [INAUDIBLE]..

01:15:18.220 --> 01:15:23.260
But really a nice example of
how gestures and animations

01:15:23.260 --> 01:15:28.420
seamlessly integrate
with each other.

