01:15:46.180 --> 01:15:49.420
I will link to resources
in the video description.

01:15:49.420 --> 01:15:52.160
We discussed the
heart of the matter.

01:15:52.160 --> 01:15:56.770
Why is this topic so [INAUDIBLE]
in React Native and the APIs

01:15:56.770 --> 01:16:00.760
and strategies to use in order
to build these very smooth user

01:16:00.760 --> 01:16:02.260
experiences.

01:16:02.260 --> 01:16:05.160
We looked at transitions,
the easiest way

01:16:05.160 --> 01:16:07.420
to animate react-native
components.

01:16:07.420 --> 01:16:11.390
And then we implemented a
simple timing function using

01:16:11.390 --> 01:16:14.500
the bare metal reanimated API.

01:16:14.500 --> 01:16:19.630
And we've built this timing
function to be interruptable,

01:16:19.630 --> 01:16:20.990
so we can pause, resume.

01:16:20.990 --> 01:16:26.530
But also we can also loop
the function across time.

01:16:26.530 --> 01:16:29.470
Then we built our first gesture.

01:16:29.470 --> 01:16:31.870
So we had these cards and
we could move them around

01:16:31.870 --> 01:16:32.650
and swipe them.

01:16:32.650 --> 01:16:38.480
And we've used the gesture API
to build a nice wallet user

01:16:38.480 --> 01:16:39.580
interaction.

01:16:39.580 --> 01:16:42.610
And finally, we've
looked at how svg

01:16:42.610 --> 01:16:47.380
can be used to seamlessly
integrate with these gestures

01:16:47.380 --> 01:16:48.820
and animations.

01:16:48.820 --> 01:16:54.850
And that allows for a range of
really fun and creative user

01:16:54.850 --> 01:16:56.830
interactions to be built.

01:16:56.830 --> 01:16:58.750
If you go on my
YouTube channel, I

01:16:58.750 --> 01:17:02.150
have dozens of
videos on this topic.

01:17:02.150 --> 01:17:04.900
For instance, in the
case of the svg example,

01:17:04.900 --> 01:17:08.950
we used functions which leverage
trigonometry behind the scene,

01:17:08.950 --> 01:17:11.370
right, where we converted
from polar coordinate system

01:17:11.370 --> 01:17:13.750
to [INAUDIBLE] and vice versa.

01:17:13.750 --> 01:17:16.120
I have videos where
I really explain

01:17:16.120 --> 01:17:18.490
the math behind the scene.

01:17:18.490 --> 01:17:21.610
And trigonometry is very
important in animations,

01:17:21.610 --> 01:17:24.250
because as soon as
you have something

01:17:24.250 --> 01:17:26.110
that has some sort
of rotation, it's

01:17:26.110 --> 01:17:27.790
going to involve trigonometry.

01:17:27.790 --> 01:17:31.360
And I have quite some
videos about this topic.

01:17:31.360 --> 01:17:34.510
And I have also
more advanced videos

01:17:34.510 --> 01:17:37.030
on the topic of svg animation.

01:17:37.030 --> 01:17:40.120
For instance, you can
use svg animations

01:17:40.120 --> 01:17:44.080
to morph from one svg
path to the other.

01:17:44.080 --> 01:17:48.100
And there is also really
interesting examples

01:17:48.100 --> 01:17:49.590
with Bezier curves.

01:17:49.590 --> 01:17:52.840
That is really-- we only
scratched the surface.

01:17:52.840 --> 01:17:55.100
There is really
tons of great things

01:17:55.100 --> 01:17:59.260
you can build using
svg animations.

01:17:59.260 --> 01:18:01.570
We looked at the
PanGestureHandler.

01:18:01.570 --> 01:18:03.700
But there are, of
course, different kinds

01:18:03.700 --> 01:18:06.550
of gestures in React Native--

01:18:06.550 --> 01:18:09.140
the tap gesture handler,
the pinch gesture handler,

01:18:09.140 --> 01:18:11.140
the rotate gesture handler.

01:18:11.140 --> 01:18:14.650
And I also have videos
on these topics.

01:18:14.650 --> 01:18:19.270
I will link to these resources
in the video description.

01:18:19.270 --> 01:18:22.510
